Lab 20: Review: Building a Resilient IdM Environment
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Deploy a resilient Identity Management (IdM) environment with multiple replicas
Configure and test replication between IdM servers
Populate the IdM environment with users and groups
Test user authentication and access across multiple replicas
Verify failover functionality when primary servers become unavailable
Understand the importance of redundancy in identity management systems
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with DNS concepts and configuration
Knowledge of LDAP and Kerberos authentication principles
Understanding of network connectivity and firewall concepts
Experience with user and group management in Linux environments
Lab Environment
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your pre-configured environment. No need to build your own virtual machines.

Your lab environment includes:

3 CentOS Stream 9 servers (idm-server1, idm-server2, idm-client1)
Pre-configured network connectivity
Required packages and dependencies
Task 1: Deploy a Resilient IdM Environment with Multiple Replicas
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the Environment
First, let's verify our lab environment and prepare the systems for IdM deployment.

Check system information and connectivity:
# Check hostname and IP configuration
hostnamectl
ip addr show
Update system packages:
sudo dnf update -y
Configure hostnames and DNS resolution:
On idm-server1 (Primary Server):

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname idm-server1.example.com
echo "192.168.1.10 idm-server1.example.com idm-server1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.11 idm-server2.example.com idm-server2" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.12 idm-client1.example.com idm-client1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
On idm-server2 (Replica Server):

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname idm-server2.example.com
echo "192.168.1.10 idm-server1.example.com idm-server1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.11 idm-server2.example.com idm-server2" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.12 idm-client1.example.com idm-client1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
On idm-client1 (Client Machine):

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname idm-client1.example.com
echo "192.168.1.10 idm-server1.example.com idm-server1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.11 idm-server2.example.com idm-server2" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.12 idm-client1.example.com idm-client1" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
Subtask 1.2: Install FreeIPA Server Packages
On both idm-server1 and idm-server2:

# Install FreeIPA server packages
sudo dnf install -y freeipa-server freeipa-server-dns

# Install additional tools for management
sudo dnf install -y bind-utils
Subtask 1.3: Configure the Primary IdM Server
On idm-server1:

Install and configure the primary IdM server:
sudo ipa-server-install \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --domain=example.com \
  --ds-password=SecurePassword123 \
  --admin-password=AdminPassword123 \
  --hostname=idm-server1.example.com \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4 \
  --unattended
Wait for installation to complete (this may take 10-15 minutes):
# Check installation status
sudo systemctl status ipa
Obtain Kerberos ticket for admin user:
kinit admin
# Enter password: AdminPassword123
Verify the installation:
# Check IPA status
ipa user-show admin

# Verify DNS resolution
nslookup idm-server1.example.com
Subtask 1.4: Configure the Replica Server
On idm-server2:

Install FreeIPA client first:
sudo ipa-client-install \
  --domain=example.com \
  --server=idm-server1.example.com \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --principal=admin \
  --password=AdminPassword123 \
  --hostname=idm-server2.example.com \
  --unattended
Promote client to replica server:
sudo ipa-replica-install \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4 \
  --unattended
Verify replica installation:
# Obtain Kerberos ticket
kinit admin
# Enter password: AdminPassword123

# Check replica status
ipa server-show idm-server2.example.com
Subtask 1.5: Configure the IdM Client
On idm-client1:

Install FreeIPA client packages:
sudo dnf install -y freeipa-client
Join the client to the IdM domain:
sudo ipa-client-install \
  --domain=example.com \
  --server=idm-server1.example.com \
  --server=idm-server2.example.com \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --principal=admin \
  --password=AdminPassword123 \
  --hostname=idm-client1.example.com \
  --unattended
Verify client installation:
# Test Kerberos authentication
kinit admin
# Enter password: AdminPassword123

# Verify client can communicate with servers
ipa user-show admin
Task 2: Populate the Environment with Users and Groups
Subtask 2.1: Create Organizational Units and Groups
On idm-server1 (or any server with admin access):

Create groups for different departments:
# Obtain admin credentials
kinit admin

# Create IT department group
ipa group-add it-department --desc="IT Department Staff"

# Create HR department group
ipa group-add hr-department --desc="Human Resources Department"

# Create Finance department group
ipa group-add finance-department --desc="Finance Department Staff"

# Create managers group
ipa group-add managers --desc="Department Managers"

# Verify groups were created
ipa group-find
Subtask 2.2: Create User Accounts
Create IT department users:
# Create IT users
ipa user-add jsmith \
  --first=John \
  --last=Smith \
  --email=jsmith@example.com \
  --password

ipa user-add mjohnson \
  --first=Mary \
  --last=Johnson \
  --email=mjohnson@example.com \
  --password

# Add users to IT department group
ipa group-add-member it-department --users=jsmith,mjohnson
Create HR department users:
# Create HR users
ipa user-add bwilson \
  --first=Bob \
  --last=Wilson \
  --email=bwilson@example.com \
  --password

ipa user-add sdavis \
  --first=Sarah \
  --last=Davis \
  --email=sdavis@example.com \
  --password

# Add users to HR department group
ipa group-add-member hr-department --users=bwilson,sdavis
Create Finance department users:
# Create Finance users
ipa user-add rgarcia \
  --first=Robert \
  --last=Garcia \
  --email=rgarcia@example.com \
  --password

# Add user to Finance department group
ipa group-add-member finance-department --users=rgarcia

# Make Robert a manager
ipa group-add-member managers --users=rgarcia
Subtask 2.3: Configure Password Policies
Create custom password policy for managers:
# Create password policy for managers
ipa pwpolicy-add managers \
  --minlength=12 \
  --minclasses=3 \
  --maxlife=60 \
  --history=5

# Verify password policies
ipa pwpolicy-find
Subtask 2.4: Create Host-Based Access Control (HBAC) Rules
Create HBAC rules for different access levels:
# Create HBAC rule for IT department
ipa hbacrule-add it-server-access \
  --desc="Allow IT department access to servers"

# Add IT group to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user it-server-access --groups=it-department

# Create HBAC rule for managers
ipa hbacrule-add manager-access \
  --desc="Allow managers access to all systems"

# Add managers group to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user manager-access --groups=managers

# Verify HBAC rules
ipa hbacrule-find
Task 3: Test User Access, Replication, and Failover Functionality
Subtask 3.1: Test User Authentication
Test user authentication on the client machine:
On idm-client1:

# Test authentication for different users
kinit jsmith
# Enter the password you set for jsmith

# Verify ticket
klist

# Test another user
kinit bwilson
# Enter the password you set for bwilson

# Verify ticket
klist
Test SSH access with IdM users:
# Enable SSH access for IdM users
sudo authselect select sssd with-mkhomedir --force

# Test SSH login (from another terminal or session)
ssh jsmith@idm-client1.example.com
Subtask 3.2: Test Replication Between Servers
Create a user on the primary server and verify replication:
On idm-server1:

# Create a test user
kinit admin
ipa user-add testuser \
  --first=Test \
  --last=User \
  --email=testuser@example.com \
  --password
Verify the user exists on the replica server:
On idm-server2:

# Check if user replicated
kinit admin
ipa user-show testuser

# Check replication status
ipa-replica-manage list
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Test bidirectional replication:
On idm-server2:

# Create a user on replica
ipa user-add replica-user \
  --first=Replica \
  --last=User \
  --email=replica-user@example.com \
  --password
On idm-server1:

# Verify user replicated back
ipa user-show replica-user
Subtask 3.3: Test Failover Functionality
Test client failover when primary server is unavailable:
On idm-server1:

# Simulate server failure by stopping services
sudo systemctl stop ipa
Test authentication on client during failover:
On idm-client1:

# Clear existing tickets
kdestroy

# Test authentication (should failover to replica)
kinit jsmith
# This should work using idm-server2

# Verify which server is being used
ipa user-show jsmith
Monitor failover in logs:
# Check SSSD logs for failover
sudo tail -f /var/log/sssd/sssd_example.com.log
Subtask 3.4: Test Service Recovery
Restart the primary server and test recovery:
On idm-server1:

# Restart IPA services
sudo systemctl start ipa
sudo systemctl status ipa
Verify replication resumes:
# Check replication agreements
kinit admin
ipa-replica-manage list
ipa topologysegment-find domain
Subtask 3.5: Performance and Load Testing
Create multiple users for load testing:
# Script to create multiple test users
for i in {1..10}; do
  ipa user-add testuser$i \
    --first=Test$i \
    --last=User$i \
    --email=testuser$i@example.com \
    --password
done
Test concurrent authentication:
On idm-client1:

# Create a simple load test script
cat > load_test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
for i in {1..5}; do
  (
    echo "Testing user testuser$i"
    kinit testuser$i << EOL
TestPassword123
EOL
    klist
    kdestroy
  ) &
done
wait
EOF

chmod +x load_test.sh
./load_test.sh
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
DNS Resolution Problems:
# Check DNS configuration
nslookup idm-server1.example.com
dig @idm-server1.example.com example.com

# Fix DNS if needed
sudo systemctl restart named
Replication Issues:
# Check replication status
ipa-replica-manage list
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv

# Force replication
ipa-replica-manage force-sync --from idm-server1.example.com
Authentication Failures:
# Check SSSD status
sudo systemctl status sssd
sudo sss_cache -E

# Check Kerberos configuration
klist -k /etc/krb5.keytab
Service Startup Issues:
# Check IPA service status
sudo ipactl status

# Restart all IPA services
sudo ipactl restart
Verification Commands
Use these commands to verify your environment is working correctly:

# Check server status
ipa server-find

# Verify replication topology
ipa topologysegment-find domain

# Check user and group information
ipa user-find
ipa group-find

# Test DNS resolution
nslookup _ldap._tcp.example.com

# Verify certificates
ipa cert-find

# Check HBAC rules
ipa hbacrule-find --all
